{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YLsrZVDfRMFn5QpxmfKVTQ","type":"tool_result","content":"=== sec2 225-240, 340-360 ===\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\nFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them; every\nnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}\n(run under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and\ncomputes in \\(y\\), so that no \\(n\\) beyond floating-point range is ever\nformed). The crossing at 50 is a fact about integers:\n\\(\\rho(49)/49 = 0.9994\\) and \\(\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027\\). In the limit\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\), since \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\): the exponent is\n\\(o(n)\\), and that is a theorem in our companion Lean\n\\leanref{rho-little-o}. Both facts are true at once, and the second is\nthe one the title is about: the function is \\(o(n)\\), and it is above\n\\(n\\) on every size anyone will ever write down.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The verified exponent relative to \\(n\\). \\(\\rhon = n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}\\),\n\\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} = O(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at\n\\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), the whole bound is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone.\nThat is a deterministic \\(2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) algorithm\nfor \\#\\(k\\)-SAT in the standard sense, and therefore for \\(k\\)-SAT; it\nrefutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001} as\nstated. The statement reads as the manuscript's verified form, the\nweaker exponent of its Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without\nthe \\(n \\ge 20\\) proviso. We found no field, definition or quantifier\nthrough which the statement could be kernel-true and mean something\nweaker than this; the audit it summarises is\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement\\_audit.md}. Our own\nrestatement of the statement as a Lean \\texttt{Prop}, character for\ncharacter over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is\n\\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved and never an axiom there\n(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}; Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}\n  is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times\n=== sec5 head and tail ===\n% Independent replay -- drafted by loop0015 item 01 (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is read from paper3/replay/logs/ (regenerate: re-run\n% ~/dev/untrusted/replay/run_sandbox.sh as paper3/replay/README.md records;\n% the counts below come from `grep -c` on the copied logs, see\n% paper3/replay/logs/run2/counts.txt).\n\\section{Independent replay}\n\\label{sec:replay}\n\nThe repository holds no recorded \\texttt{\\#print axioms} output for the\nfamily, no continuous integration that builds its Lean, and\n\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the comparator's JSON\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We therefore built the solution's import\nclosure ourselves, in isolation, at the toolchain the repository pins, and\nrecorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was run,\nwhere, and what came out; the scripts are quoted verbatim in\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}, and the logs are under\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}.\n\n\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\n\\texttt{bwrap} (bubblewrap) sandbox on the project's machine: the system\ndirectories bound read-only, a private \\texttt{tmpfs} home with nothing of\nthe real home or of this repository visible, separate PID, IPC and UTS\nnamespaces, the whole sandbox confined to eight cores\n(\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling priority, and the\nnetwork on, because the repository's \\texttt{lakefile} clones its 41\ndependencies at build time and the Mathlib object files come from the\nMathlib cache. Inside, \\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home\nand fetched the pinned toolchain from the repository's own\n...\n19:\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n49:\\subsection{What came out}\n97:\\subsection{The second kernel}\n128:\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned toolchain\n  (\\texttt{v4.34.1}) and upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; it\n  moves the proof's trust from ``OpenAI's unpublished build'' to ``a\n  build we ran'', not from the Lean kernel to anything else.\n  The second kernel removes the Lean kernel from the list for the\n  theorem's dependencies as exported, but adds \\texttt{lean4export}\n  (that it exports what the environment holds) and \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib}\n  (that it checks what it parses) to it; two independent checkers\n  agreeing is evidence, not a proof that either is correct.\n\\item It is not the comparator run. The comparator checks that the\n  solution's exported statement matches the challenge's by\n  \\texttt{lean4export}; we did not run that tool chain, and the\n  statement-level match the paper relies on is the textual one of\n  Section~\\ref{sec:proof} (the 35 definitions and the theorem statement,\n  identical character for character after whitespace normalisation, two\n  of them up to the \\texttt{noncomputable} keyword).\n\\item The counts above are counts of lines in one build's log on one\n  machine with the named versions; a different Lake would print different\n  job totals (the 12,014 includes the cached Mathlib modules), and a\n  different machine a different time.\n\\item Nothing here bears on what the theorem \\emph{says}\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) or on the manuscript's unverified\n  exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}); a replayed proof of a weak bound\n  is a replayed proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n=== refs.bib keys ===\n10:@misc{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026,\n19:@article{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001,\n29:@article{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001,\n39:@misc{SienickiSienicki2026,\n54:@misc{LeanComparator,\n65:@misc{Nanoda,\n83:@techreport{Dinur2016,\n93:@inproceedings{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017,\n105:@article{Chalermsook2020,\n114:@inproceedings{Carmosino2016,\n124:@article{Dell2014,\n134:@article{Cygan2016,\n143:@inproceedings{Williams2016,\n164:@inproceedings{Biere2024CaDiCaL,\n177:@inproceedings{Ignatiev2018PySAT,\n190:@inproceedings{Sharma2019Ganak,\n200:@inproceedings{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\n212:@inproceedings{Soos2019BIRD,\n222:@article{Selman1996Hard,\n233:@article{Mertens2006Threshold,\n=== README regenerate 77-100 ===\n## How to regenerate every number\n\n*(Filled in by the items, section by section. Until an item fills its\nsection: the closure counts come from `python3 -I\nreports/scratch/eth_audit/closure.py` and `defdiff.py`, read-only on the\nclone.)*\n\n- **Section 2, the statement** (item 02): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py` prints the `ρ(n)/n` table (Table 2.1),\n  the first integer above 1 (`n = 50`: `ρ(49)/49 = 0.9994`,\n  `ρ(50)/50 = 1.0027`), the peak (`16/e² = 2.1654` at `ln(n+16) = e⁴`,\n  `n ≈ 10^23.71`) and the return below 1 (`ln(n+16) = 5503.7`,\n  `n ≈ e^5504 ≈ 10^2390`), and writes `latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex` and\n  `latex/figures/runtime_scale.{pdf,png}` (Figure 2.2). `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/rho_over_n.py` draws the central figure (Figure 2.1, two\n  panels, with the manuscript's two unverified exponents beneath) and\n  writes `latex/tables/rho_over_n.csv`. The encoding lengths quoted in\n  §2.1 (`L = 1,006,223` at `n, m, k = 125, 500, 4`; `47.9 M` at\n  `1000, 4000, 3`) come from `python3 -I\n  reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py`. Every comparator line\n  number is at clone commit `4eaee875`; every manuscript quote gives the\n  page of `paper.pdf` and the line of `reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt`.\n- **Section 3, what the manuscript claims beyond the statement** (item 03):\n  `python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py` checks every page, line and\n=== scripts ===\n__pycache__\nbeyond_claims.py\nconsequences.py\npractitioner.py\nproof_inspection.py\nrho_over_n.py\nruntime_scale.py\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Section 6 of paper 3: what follows, in general terms -- the hypotheses the\nverified statement refutes, and what a lower bound derived from them loses.\n\nRegenerates every location Section 6 cites and fails loudly if any has drifted:\n\n  * for each hypothesis the manuscript's §1.1.1 / §1.1.2 states (ETH, SETH,\n    Gap-ETH and its randomised form, NSETH, #ETH, the randomised ETH and SETH\n    it names by citation), the page of paper.pdf and the line range of the\n    committed extract reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds\n    before the line + 1), with a quoted fragment that must occur in that range,\n    and the bracket key of the manuscript's own source for it;\n  * the manuscript's refutation paragraph (p. 4, lines 187-191), its #ETH\n    sentence (p. 5, lines 211-212), its §1.1.3 sentences on what the\n    refutation removes and what it does not touch (p. 5, lines 226-227,\n    229-236), the transfer caveat (p. 2, lines 95-99) and the one positive\n    transfer (p. 5, lines 237-243);\n  * the manuscript's bibliography entries the paper's refs.bib copies for the\n    hypotheses' sources (lines 3686-3811 of the extract), so that every entry\n    added by item 05 has a located origin;\n  * the comparator lines Section 6 relies on (the answer bit, the count tape,\n    the time bound, the width predicate), read from the clone at commit\n    4eaee875 with open() only; nothing of the clone is imported or run;\n  * two arithmetic facts used in the text: the number of clauses of width at\n    most k over n variables is at most sum_{j<=k} C(2n, j), so L is polynomial\n    in n alone (the values at n = 125, k = 3 and at n = 1000, k = 3), and\n    rho(n)/n -> 0 (the value at a few n, from the same formula as\n    runtime_scale.py).\n\nWrites paper3/latex/tables/hypotheses.tex (Table 6.1: hypothesis, where the\nmanuscript states it, its source, the quantifier form, what in the verified\nstatement negates it).\n\nRun:  python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py           (from the repository root)\n      python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py --check   (verify only, write nothing)\nExit status is non-zero if any fragment, page or line has moved.\n\"\"\"\nimport math\nimport os\nimport sys\n=== subexp report headings ===\n9:## 0. Summary\n14:## 1. The audit and the kill check (item 01, 2026-11-04)\n399:## 2. The formal frame: `SubexpCount`, `PathwidthSubexp`, `PathwidthSubexpOn` (item 02, 2026-11-04)\n408:### 2.1 The decision: the machine model is copied verbatim\n466:### 2.2 The hypothesis, exactly\n498:### 2.3 The target, in the same model\n565:### 2.4 Glue between the two: the verified bound has the target's shape\n589:### 2.5 What a transfer theorem will look like, given this frame\n617:### 2.6 Checks\n641:### 2.7 What this does not claim\n661:### 2.8 Regenerate\n668:## 3. The reduction catalogue: every reduction into pathwidth or vertex separation on record, with its blow-up (item 03, 2026-11-04)\n988:## 4. Classes with `O(n)`-bit layouts? Cobipartite, split, maximum degree 3, cocomparability, the clique-tree covers (item 04, 2026-11-04)\n1540:## 5. The chain count as an algebraic object (item 05, 2026-11-04)\n2017:## 6. Algorithms from the counting theorem for problems on graphs of linear pathwidth: the crossover (item 06, 2026-11-04)\n2527:## 7. New exact algorithms by exact linear transformation, unconditional (item 07, 2026-11-05)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f52cb7ed-9351-4bd6-99f1-a93db5c9356a","uuid":"87c83fc2-80af-4845-b630-676fce9c5abe","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:27:57.607Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== sec2 225-240, 340-360 ===\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\nFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them; every\nnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}\n(run under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and\ncomputes in \\(y\\), so that no \\(n\\) beyond floating-point range is ever\nformed). The crossing at 50 is a fact about integers:\n\\(\\rho(49)/49 = 0.9994\\) and \\(\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027\\). In the limit\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\), since \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\): the exponent is\n\\(o(n)\\), and that is a theorem in our companion Lean\n\\leanref{rho-little-o}. Both facts are true at once, and the second is\nthe one the title is about: the function is \\(o(n)\\), and it is above\n\\(n\\) on every size anyone will ever write down.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The verified exponent relative to \\(n\\). \\(\\rhon = n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}\\),\n\\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} = O(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at\n\\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), the whole bound is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone.\nThat is a deterministic \\(2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) algorithm\nfor \\#\\(k\\)-SAT in the standard sense, and therefore for \\(k\\)-SAT; it\nrefutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001} as\nstated. The statement reads as the manuscript's verified form, the\nweaker exponent of its Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without\nthe \\(n \\ge 20\\) proviso. We found no field, definition or quantifier\nthrough which the statement could be kernel-true and mean something\nweaker than this; the audit it summarises is\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement\\_audit.md}. Our own\nrestatement of the statement as a Lean \\texttt{Prop}, character for\ncharacter over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is\n\\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved and never an axiom there\n(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}; Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}\n  is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times\n=== sec5 head and tail ===\n% Independent replay -- drafted by loop0015 item 01 (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is read from paper3/replay/logs/ (regenerate: re-run\n% ~/dev/untrusted/replay/run_sandbox.sh as paper3/replay/README.md records;\n% the counts below come from `grep -c` on the copied logs, see\n% paper3/replay/logs/run2/counts.txt).\n\\section{Independent replay}\n\\label{sec:replay}\n\nThe repository holds no recorded \\texttt{\\#print axioms} output for the\nfamily, no continuous integration that builds its Lean, and\n\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the comparator's JSON\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We therefore built the solution's import\nclosure ourselves, in isolation, at the toolchain the repository pins, and\nrecorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was run,\nwhere, and what came out; the scripts are quoted verbatim in\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}, and the logs are under\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}.\n\n\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\n\\texttt{bwrap} (bubblewrap) sandbox on the project's machine: the system\ndirectories bound read-only, a private \\texttt{tmpfs} home with nothing of\nthe real home or of this repository visible, separate PID, IPC and UTS\nnamespaces, the whole sandbox confined to eight cores\n(\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling priority, and the\nnetwork on, because the repository's \\texttt{lakefile} clones its 41\ndependencies at build time and the Mathlib object files come from the\nMathlib cache. Inside, \\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home\nand fetched the pinned toolchain from the repository's own\n...\n19:\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n49:\\subsection{What came out}\n97:\\subsection{The second kernel}\n128:\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned toolchain\n  (\\texttt{v4.34.1}) and upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; it\n  moves the proof's trust from ``OpenAI's unpublished build'' to ``a\n  build we ran'', not from the Lean kernel to anything else.\n  The second kernel removes the Lean kernel from the list for the\n  theorem's dependencies as exported, but adds \\texttt{lean4export}\n  (that it exports what the environment holds) and \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib}\n  (that it checks what it parses) to it; two independent checkers\n  agreeing is evidence, not a proof that either is correct.\n\\item It is not the comparator run. The comparator checks that the\n  solution's exported statement matches the challenge's by\n  \\texttt{lean4export}; we did not run that tool chain, and the\n  statement-level match the paper relies on is the textual one of\n  Section~\\ref{sec:proof} (the 35 definitions and the theorem statement,\n  identical character for character after whitespace normalisation, two\n  of them up to the \\texttt{noncomputable} keyword).\n\\item The counts above are counts of lines in one build's log on one\n  machine with the named versions; a different Lake would print different\n  job totals (the 12,014 includes the cached Mathlib modules), and a\n  different machine a different time.\n\\item Nothing here bears on what the theorem \\emph{says}\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) or on the manuscript's unverified\n  exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}); a replayed proof of a weak bound\n  is a replayed proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n=== refs.bib keys ===\n10:@misc{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026,\n19:@article{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001,\n29:@article{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001,\n39:@misc{SienickiSienicki2026,\n54:@misc{LeanComparator,\n65:@misc{Nanoda,\n83:@techreport{Dinur2016,\n93:@inproceedings{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017,\n105:@article{Chalermsook2020,\n114:@inproceedings{Carmosino2016,\n124:@article{Dell2014,\n134:@article{Cygan2016,\n143:@inproceedings{Williams2016,\n164:@inproceedings{Biere2024CaDiCaL,\n177:@inproceedings{Ignatiev2018PySAT,\n190:@inproceedings{Sharma2019Ganak,\n200:@inproceedings{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\n212:@inproceedings{Soos2019BIRD,\n222:@article{Selman1996Hard,\n233:@article{Mertens2006Threshold,\n=== README regenerate 77-100 ===\n## How to regenerate every number\n\n*(Filled in by the items, section by section. Until an item fills its\nsection: the closure counts come from `python3 -I\nreports/scratch/eth_audit/closure.py` and `defdiff.py`, read-only on the\nclone.)*\n\n- **Section 2, the statement** (item 02): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py` prints the `ρ(n)/n` table (Table 2.1),\n  the first integer above 1 (`n = 50`: `ρ(49)/49 = 0.9994`,\n  `ρ(50)/50 = 1.0027`), the peak (`16/e² = 2.1654` at `ln(n+16) = e⁴`,\n  `n ≈ 10^23.71`) and the return below 1 (`ln(n+16) = 5503.7`,\n  `n ≈ e^5504 ≈ 10^2390`), and writes `latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex` and\n  `latex/figures/runtime_scale.{pdf,png}` (Figure 2.2). `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/rho_over_n.py` draws the central figure (Figure 2.1, two\n  panels, with the manuscript's two unverified exponents beneath) and\n  writes `latex/tables/rho_over_n.csv`. The encoding lengths quoted in\n  §2.1 (`L = 1,006,223` at `n, m, k = 125, 500, 4`; `47.9 M` at\n  `1000, 4000, 3`) come from `python3 -I\n  reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py`. Every comparator line\n  number is at clone commit `4eaee875`; every manuscript quote gives the\n  page of `paper.pdf` and the line of `reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt`.\n- **Section 3, what the manuscript claims beyond the statement** (item 03):\