{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjV8mQZounsM8VsRP4dRHM","type":"tool_result","content":"--\n971-[32] [33 <./figures/practitioner.pdf>] [34]) (./sec8_lean.tex\n972:Overfull \\hbox (1.72417pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 26--34\n973-[]\\T1/pplx/m/n/10.95 The files are $\\T1/pcr/m/n/10.95 lean / MOSPFormalization \n974-/ SubexpPathwidth / PrimitiveMachine . lean$\n975- []\n--\n977-\n978:Overfull \\hbox (0.72191pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 26--34\n979-\\T1/pplx/m/n/10.95 owner chose to leave the files where they are rather than mo\n980-ve them ($\\T1/pcr/m/n/10.95 paper3 / ETH \\ _AMBIGUOUS .\n981- []\n--\n983-\n984:Overfull \\hbox (10.97125pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 38--48\n985-\\T1/pplx/m/n/10.95 The com-para-tor file of fam-ily 378, $\\T1/pcr/m/n/10.95 lea\n986-n / ComparatorChallenges / SubexponentialSAT . lean$\\T1/pplx/m/n/10.95 ,\n987- []\n--\n990-) (./sec12_ack.tex) (./main.bbl [38] [39]) (./secA_appendix_lean.tex\n991:Overfull \\hbox (33.88957pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 6--17\n992-\\T1/pplx/m/n/10.95 Every Lean name the pa-per cites, with its file un-der $\\T1/\n993-pcr/m/n/10.95 lean / MOSPFormalization / SubexpPathwidth/$\n994- []\n--\n=== section 8 text ===\n8      The statement in our Lean\nSections 2 and 4 read OpenAI’s statement in OpenAI’s files. This section describes the one thing\nwe wrote in Lean ourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by the same\nkernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence this paper says about “the\nverified statement” is pinned to a named object that the Lean kernel has elaborated, and anyone\nwho wants to use the statement as a hypothesis later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved\nabout the hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy and its audit show.\n    Two words first. In Lean a Prop is a sentence that may be true or false; a def gives a sentence a\nname without asserting it; a theorem asserts a sentence and carries its proof; an axiom asserts one\nwithout a proof. Our restatement is a def. It names the sentence and asserts nothing.\n    The files are lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean\nand lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean, written on 2026-11-04 for\nan earlier study of ours on a different question. The directory carries that study’s name. The\nowner chose to leave the files where they are rather than move them (paper3/ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.\nMD, S3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the copied model, the restatement,\nand six small lemmas about them, all listed in Appendix A.\n\n\n\n\n                                                 35\n\f8.1   The copy: definitions only\nThe comparator file of family 378, lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean,\nhas 220 lines. Lines 1–190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the formulas, their canon-\nical encoding as a bit string, the multitape machine, its step and its starting configuration, the\nrunning-time bound runtimeScale and the wrapper around it, the count word, and the located\nconfiguration that records which tape cells a run has visited. Lines 191–219 state the theorem\nexact_count_and_sat and end it with sorry, the placeholder that a solution must replace.\nLine 220 closes the last namespace.\n    Our PrimitiveMachine.lean is lines 1–190 and 220, and nothing else: after a thirty-line\nheader that records the source, the commit, the file’s hash and the Apache licence, the file is\nthe copy (Appendix A, A.1). The header says so, and a script checks it. paper3/scripts/\nlean\\_rediff.py reads both files with open() only, takes everything in ours from the line\nimport Mathlib on, and compares it byte for byte with the clone’s lines 1–190 followed by line\n220. The diff is empty: 191 lines on each side, identical. The script also checks the clone file’s\nSHA-256 against the hash the header records, and that the clone is at commit 4eaee875. The\nlog is paper3/lean/rediff.log.\n    So the copy holds the theorem’s vocabulary and not the theorem. The sorry at line 218 is\noutside the copied range, and our file has no sorry and no axiom declaration anywhere (the\nsame script counts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the copy is a\ndefinition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be false; what the definitions mean is\nSection 2’s subject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n\n8.2   The restatement\nFrame.lean defines\n                                  SubexpCount (k : N) : Prop\nas the body of the comparator theorem for width k, with the theorem’s two binders handled\nthus: k is the argument of the def, and the hypothesis 2 ≤ k is carried by a second def,\nExactCountAndSat, which says “for every k ≥ 2, SubexpCount k” (Appendix A, A.2), (Ap-\npendix A, A.3). In words, SubexpCount k is the sentence of Section 2: one fixed machine,\non every bounded-width formula, writes the exact number of satisfying assignments, answers\nwhether there is one, runs within the bound built from runtimeScale, and visits only polyno-\nmially many cells.\n     The same script checks that the body is the comparator’s. It takes the 23 lines after def\nSubexpCount (k : N) : Prop := in our file (lines 85–107) and the 23 lines after the\ntheorem’s binders in the clone (lines 195–217, with the closing := by removed), and compares\nthem in two ways: with every run of white space collapsed to one space, and line by line with\nthe indentation stripped. Both comparisons find the two texts identical, 1,073 characters. The\nonly difference is that each of our lines starts two spaces further left, because a def’s body sits\ntwo columns inside a theorem’s type. We call this “character for character”; it is a textual match,\nby the same method as Section 4’s, and it rests on the file we copied from being the comparator\nfile at the commit we name.\n     Neither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of SubexpCount k for k ≥ 2\nrests on OpenAI’s proof and on Sections 4 and 5, not on anything in our library. Our library\nwould be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n\n                                                36\n\f8.3     What is proved, and what it means in words\nSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines (Appendix A, A.4)–(Appendix A,\nA.8). None of them says anything about any machine’s running time or about whether the\nhypothesis is true.\n\n      • A machine for width k is a machine for every smaller width. SubexpCount.anti: if\n        SubexpCount k holds and k′ ≤ k then SubexpCount k′ holds, with the same machine and\n        the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most k′ literals has at most k.\n        ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount unpacks the “for every k ≥ 2” form.\n\n      • The bound is what it is. runtimeBound_eq: the wrapper runtimeBound C A d L n un-\n        folds, by definition, to C ( L + 1)d 2 A·ρ(n) , the formula Section 2 reads. The proof is rfl: the\n        two sides are the same term.\n                                                                                     √\n      • The exponent is o (n). tendsto_log_sq_div_sqrt says (ln y)2 / y → 0 as y → ∞,\n        and runtimeScale_isLittleO says that ρ(n)/n → 0 as n → ∞, that is, ρ(n) = o (n)\n        (Appendix A, A.6). This is the limit fact that Sections 2, 3 and 6 cite: in the limit the\n        exponent is smaller than n by any factor one names. It is true at the same time as the\n        title’s fact that ρ(n) > n for every 50 ≤ n < e5504 , and the Lean says nothing about where\n        the limit begins to bite. isSubexpExponent_runtimeScale is the same statement for\n        A · ρ(n), for every constant A (Appendix A, A.7).\n\n      • The time bound is a bound on the plain machine. Trace.config_of_locatedStep:\n        the comparator states its time bound on the located step, which records visited cells beside\n        each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the plain step with the\n        same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on the machine itself (Appendix A, A.8).\n        The one-line LocatedCfg.monitor_config is its helper.\n\n8.4     The axiom audit\nLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses. Three are standard in Math-\nlib and in every proof of Sections 4 and 5: propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. A\nfourth, sorryAx, appears exactly when a proof was left out with sorry. The audit paper3/\nlean/axiom\\_check.lean prints the axioms of 21 declarations: the ten copied definitions the\nstatement rests on, the two defs, and the nine lemmas and helper definitions, in the pattern of\nour first paper’s audit. It is run from our library’s directory as\ncd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean \\\n  > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log\nand the log is committed. The result: nineteen lines on exactly the three standard axioms; Trace\non no axiom at all (it is an inductive definition); countWord on two of the three; sorryAx on\nnone. The library it runs against is built with Lean 4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib at 1192d624, neither\nof which is what the clone pins (Lean 4.34.1, Mathlib d13f23b7, Section 4). That the copied\nmodel elaborates unchanged against a different toolchain and a different Mathlib revision is\nsmall evidence that it rests on nothing patched or private, and no more than that. Our whole\nlibrary builds with no sorry and no axiom declaration, which the loop’s gate checks after every\nsession.\n\n                                                    37\n\f8.5     What this does not claim\n      • SubexpCount k is a def of a Prop, never proved here. Its truth is OpenAI’s theorem,\n        replayed in Section 5; our library neither proves it nor assumes it. Nothing in this section\n        makes the refutation more or less certain.\n\n      • The copy is definitions only. The proof is not copied, and could not be: it is 3,091 modules\n        (Section 4). What is copied is the vocabulary of the statement, so that the restatement reads\n        as the comparator does.\n\n      • No Lean computation evaluates the exponent. runtimeScale is defined over the real\n        numbers with real logarithms and is marked noncomputable; no #eval can print ρ(50)\n        or ρ(50)/50. Every number in Section 2 comes from a Python script, not from Lean, and\n        the Lean proves only the limit.\n\n      • The match is textual. “Character for character” means the normalised texts are equal; it is\n        not a check at the level of lean4export, and it rests on the clone being the repository at\n        the commit named.\n\n      • The lemmas are trivial on purpose. They fix the shape of the bound and the limit of the\n        exponent, and nothing else. No theorem about any machine’s running time, about the\n        constants, or about the manuscript’s sharper exponents is stated, because none is verified.\n\n      • The directory name is not a citation. The files sit in a directory named for another study\n        of ours; nothing from that study enters this paper, and the owner’s decision to leave them\n        in place is recorded, not argued.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f52cb7ed-9351-4bd6-99f1-a93db5c9356a","uuid":"bb95cda0-0800-4eb3-9b3e-76260ca6ccd5","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:33:36.328Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"--\n971-[32] [33 <./figures/practitioner.pdf>] [34]) (./sec8_lean.tex\n972:Overfull \\hbox (1.72417pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 26--34\n973-[]\\T1/pplx/m/n/10.95 The files are $\\T1/pcr/m/n/10.95 lean / MOSPFormalization \n974-/ SubexpPathwidth / PrimitiveMachine . lean$\n975- []\n--\n977-\n978:Overfull \\hbox (0.72191pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 26--34\n979-\\T1/pplx/m/n/10.95 owner chose to leave the files where they are rather than mo\n980-ve them ($\\T1/pcr/m/n/10.95 paper3 / ETH \\ _AMBIGUOUS .\n981- []\n